pyDEXPI Neo4j Browser stylesheet (GRASS). Visualisation only - it never changes
graph data. Import it with the :style command in Neo4j Browser. Full
documentation: docs/mkdocs/neo4j_export.md in the pyDEXPI repository.

DO NOT ADD COMMENTS ABOVE OR BETWEEN THE RULES, AND NO QUOTES OR BRACES BELOW.
The Neo4j Browser GRASS parser has no comment syntax; any text before a rule is
merged into that rule selector and silently disables it. This trailing block is
safe only because it follows the final closing brace and contains no quote or
brace characters, which would otherwise resume parsing and create a junk rule.
For the same reason, values must be quoted with single quotes, never double
quotes, and must never contain a colon.

Visual grammar. Blue is physical equipment, cyan is piping components, teal and
green is piping connectivity, orange and yellow is measurement and
instrumentation, purple is actuation and control, grey is structural and helper
objects, and a red-orange border marks safety-relevant items. Fills are light
with darker borders and dark text, so the result reads closer to an engineering
drawing than to a set of saturated circles. These colours are a navigation aid
only. They are not a normative DEXPI or ISA colour standard.

Styling targets semantic categories rather than individual DEXPI classes.
Inheritance labels such as DexpiElement, DexpiNode and ConceptualObject are
styled neutral grey on purpose: they exist so every node keeps a caption and a
defined colour, and they must never outrank a semantic category.

Rules are ordered generic to specific on purpose. Nodes carry their whole DEXPI
inheritance chain as labels, every matching rule is applied in file order, and
the last match wins per property - so reordering these rules changes which
style you see. The neutral fallback rules therefore come first and the semantic
category rules last.

Every node rule repeats the caption deliberately. Neo4j Browser generates its
own caption rule for any node not covered by a label-scoped rule that sets one,
and its guess picks the first property whose name ends in name - which can be
deviceTypeName, tagName or pipingComponentName rather than displayName.

Every caption is a single token - either the type token or one property
placeholder. The rebuilt Neo4j Browser, default on localhost since 2025.05 and
on Aura from 2025.08, resolves a caption as one property name and renders
nothing at all for a template holding two placeholders or literal text. The
Browser style editor itself can only ever build single-token captions, so this
is a property of the format and not a bug in one engine.

Only these properties are supported. Nodes take caption, diameter, color,
border-color, border-width, text-color-internal and font-size. Relationships
take caption, color, shaft-width, font-size, padding, text-color-external and
text-color-internal. There is no verified dashed, dotted, line-style or
stroke-dasharray support for relationships through GRASS, so measurement and
actuation edges are separated by colour and width instead.

Collapsed relationships are captioned by their display name, which pyDEXPI fills
with an engineer-facing term - Measurement, Signal, Actuation, Direct connection,
and for a pipe the class name with its line number. The nominal diameter, fluid
code and piping class are all one click away in the relationship details panel.
To caption pipes by one of those instead, set the caption in relationship.Pipe
and relationship.DirectPipingConnection to the nominalDiameterRepresentation
property, or to the lineNumber property for the bare number.

Relationship captions are also dropped when they do not fit the free space
between two nodes, computed as the centre distance minus both node radii minus
the arrow head. Larger node diameters and thicker shafts both shrink that space,
which is why the pipe rules stay moderately thick and use minimal padding.
